1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,580
Here is a snowflake.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:03,740
Turn it by a sixth of a turn,

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,260
and nothing changes.

4
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:07,900
Flip it over, and nothing changes.

5
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:11,260
It is one of the most symmetric things in nature.

6
00:00:23,365 --> 00:00:24,305
Here is you.

7
00:00:24,325 --> 00:00:26,705
Your heart sits on the left.

8
00:00:26,885 --> 00:00:29,825
Your face is a little different on each side.

9
00:00:29,925 --> 00:00:32,865
And the molecules in your body all twist the same

10
00:00:32,885 --> 00:00:33,885
way.

11
00:00:44,327 --> 00:00:46,067
Here is the strange part.

12
00:00:46,327 --> 00:00:49,267
If the universe had kept every symmetry it started

13
00:00:49,287 --> 00:00:52,707
with, there would be no snowflake and no you.

14
00:00:52,967 --> 00:00:54,067
Nothing at all.

15
00:00:54,407 --> 00:00:58,227
Everything you see exists because, again and again,

16
00:00:58,367 --> 00:01:00,387
a perfect symmetry broke.

17
00:01:17,149 --> 00:01:20,689
Today we follow those breaks, from the first second

18
00:01:20,709 --> 00:01:23,529
of the universe to the moment your heart moved to

19
00:01:23,549 --> 00:01:27,049
the left. Five breaks, and at the end of them,

20
00:01:27,309 --> 00:01:28,309
you.

21
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:43,660
First, what does symmetry actually mean?

22
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:47,020
Mathematicians have a beautifully simple answer.

23
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:50,060
A symmetry is something you can do to an object

24
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,620
that leaves it looking exactly the same.

25
00:02:05,388 --> 00:02:06,328
Take a square.

26
00:02:06,348 --> 00:02:09,448
Turn it a quarter turn: same square.

27
00:02:09,708 --> 00:02:12,648
Flip it across a diagonal: same square.

28
00:02:12,828 --> 00:02:15,928
Count every move that works and you find eight.

29
00:02:16,108 --> 00:02:19,728
A circle has infinitely many: turn it by any angle

30
00:02:19,748 --> 00:02:20,748
at all.

31
00:02:36,277 --> 00:02:39,297
Three kinds of move do almost all the work.

32
00:02:39,477 --> 00:02:41,217
Reflection, a mirror.

33
00:02:41,397 --> 00:02:43,297
Rotation, a turn.

34
00:02:43,397 --> 00:02:46,977
And translation, a slide, like a wallpaper pattern

35
00:02:46,997 --> 00:02:48,497
that repeats forever.

36
00:03:01,419 --> 00:03:04,839
Notice something. The more symmetric a thing is,

37
00:03:04,859 --> 00:03:06,999
the less there is to say about it.

38
00:03:07,179 --> 00:03:10,279
A blank circle has the most symmetry and the least

39
00:03:10,299 --> 00:03:12,919
information. Hold on to that idea.

40
00:03:13,019 --> 00:03:15,239
It is the key to the whole story.

41
00:03:31,204 --> 00:03:33,024
Nature is full of it.

42
00:03:33,124 --> 00:03:36,784
A snowflake has six arms because water molecules lock

43
00:03:36,804 --> 00:03:39,184
together into a hexagonal lattice.

44
00:03:39,484 --> 00:03:42,544
And all six arms grow through the same changing air

45
00:03:42,564 --> 00:03:45,584
at the same moment, so they nearly match.

46
00:04:00,839 --> 00:04:04,179
A starfish has five-fold symmetry, although,

47
00:04:04,199 --> 00:04:06,579
as a larva, it had a left and a right

48
00:04:06,599 --> 00:04:08,259
side, just like us.

49
00:04:08,439 --> 00:04:12,099
A daisy, a sunflower, an orange cut in half:

50
00:04:12,359 --> 00:04:13,619
rotations again.

51
00:04:27,234 --> 00:04:29,294
And look at almost any animal:

52
00:04:29,394 --> 00:04:32,174
a butterfly, a fish, a tiger,

53
00:04:32,354 --> 00:04:35,134
you. One mirror line down the middle.

54
00:04:35,314 --> 00:04:39,214
Biologists call it bilateral symmetry, and over ninety-eight

55
00:04:39,234 --> 00:04:42,014
percent of known animal species share it.

56
00:04:57,496 --> 00:04:59,796
Why is nature so symmetric?

57
00:04:59,976 --> 00:05:02,036
Because the rules behind it are.

58
00:05:02,296 --> 00:05:06,036
In 1894 Pierre Curie made a simple point:

59
00:05:06,296 --> 00:05:09,156
whatever symmetry the causes have shows up in the

60
00:05:09,176 --> 00:05:12,356
effects. So if something is lopsided,

61
00:05:12,416 --> 00:05:14,996
the lopsidedness had to come from somewhere.

62
00:05:33,243 --> 00:05:35,383
Which raises the real question.

63
00:05:35,563 --> 00:05:38,983
If the laws are symmetric, where does all the asymmetry

64
00:05:39,003 --> 00:05:40,003
come from?

65
00:05:48,504 --> 00:05:52,644
In 1918 the mathematician Emmy Noether proved what

66
00:05:52,664 --> 00:05:56,004
many physicists call one of the most beautiful results

67
00:05:56,024 --> 00:05:57,124
in science.

68
00:06:06,436 --> 00:06:10,496
Do an experiment today, then the same experiment tomorrow.

69
00:06:10,596 --> 00:06:13,496
You get the same result, because the laws of physics

70
00:06:13,516 --> 00:06:15,216
do not change with time.

71
00:06:15,516 --> 00:06:16,896
That is a symmetry.

72
00:06:17,156 --> 00:06:20,656
Noether proved that this symmetry is exactly why energy

73
00:06:20,676 --> 00:06:21,776
is conserved.

74
00:06:37,795 --> 00:06:40,335
Slide the experiment across the room:

75
00:06:40,595 --> 00:06:44,175
same laws, and that is why momentum is conserved.

76
00:06:44,435 --> 00:06:46,175
Turn it to face another way:

77
00:06:46,435 --> 00:06:49,695
same laws, and that is why angular momentum,

78
00:06:49,915 --> 00:06:51,455
spin, is conserved.

79
00:07:05,967 --> 00:07:08,827
Every symmetry of the laws comes with something that

80
00:07:08,847 --> 00:07:11,387
can never be created or destroyed.

81
00:07:11,727 --> 00:07:13,947
Symmetry is not decoration.

82
00:07:14,127 --> 00:07:16,587
It is the deepest rule in physics.

83
00:07:27,921 --> 00:07:31,021
But a world that kept every symmetry would be very

84
00:07:31,041 --> 00:07:34,381
dull. Let's see what happens when one breaks.

85
00:07:42,817 --> 00:07:46,157
Break one happened in the first fraction of a second.

86
00:07:46,417 --> 00:07:50,237
The young universe made matter and antimatter in pairs:

87
00:07:50,337 --> 00:07:52,317
a perfectly symmetric deal.

88
00:07:52,657 --> 00:07:55,117
When a particle meets its antiparticle,

89
00:07:55,217 --> 00:07:57,037
both vanish into light.

90
00:08:11,929 --> 00:08:13,829
If the deal had been perfect,

91
00:08:13,929 --> 00:08:16,309
every particle would have found its partner,

92
00:08:16,489 --> 00:08:19,029
and the universe would be nothing but light.

93
00:08:19,249 --> 00:08:22,069
No atoms, no stars, nothing.

94
00:08:33,048 --> 00:08:34,548
It wasn't perfect.

95
00:08:34,648 --> 00:08:37,908
For roughly every billion particles of antimatter,

96
00:08:37,928 --> 00:08:40,788
there was about one extra particle of matter.

97
00:08:41,048 --> 00:08:42,948
The billions annihilated.

98
00:08:43,008 --> 00:08:44,708
The extra one survived.

99
00:08:44,888 --> 00:08:48,788
Every galaxy, every star, every atom in your body

100
00:08:48,808 --> 00:08:50,708
is made of those leftovers.

101
00:09:09,213 --> 00:09:12,153
Physicists first caught nature playing favourites

102
00:09:12,173 --> 00:09:16,073
in 1964, in particles called kaons.

103
00:09:16,413 --> 00:09:19,513
Exactly how the universe tipped the balance is still

104
00:09:19,533 --> 00:09:22,393
one of the biggest open questions in physics.

105
00:09:37,588 --> 00:09:41,168
Break two. Look at the oldest light there is,

106
00:09:41,308 --> 00:09:44,128
released about three hundred and eighty thousand years

107
00:09:44,148 --> 00:09:45,408
after the Big Bang.

108
00:09:45,708 --> 00:09:49,208
It is almost perfectly smooth: the same temperature

109
00:09:49,228 --> 00:09:50,688
in every direction.

110
00:10:04,506 --> 00:10:08,566
Almost. The differences are about one part in a hundred

111
00:10:08,586 --> 00:10:11,846
thousand, like ripples a hundredth of a millimetre

112
00:10:11,866 --> 00:10:14,246
high on a pond a metre deep.

113
00:10:24,684 --> 00:10:26,504
Gravity loves a lump.

114
00:10:26,684 --> 00:10:29,784
A slightly denser spot pulls in a little more,

115
00:10:29,844 --> 00:10:31,624
which makes it denser still.

116
00:10:31,884 --> 00:10:35,144
Over billions of years those tiny ripples grew into

117
00:10:35,164 --> 00:10:37,784
galaxies, stars and planets.

118
00:10:37,964 --> 00:10:41,064
A perfectly smooth universe would have stayed smooth

119
00:10:41,084 --> 00:10:42,084
forever.

120
00:11:01,209 --> 00:11:03,989
Break three. Hold up your hands.

121
00:11:04,089 --> 00:11:07,309
They are mirror images, but you cannot turn one into

122
00:11:07,329 --> 00:11:09,989
the other. Many molecules are like that:

123
00:11:10,169 --> 00:11:13,429
they come in a left-handed and a right-handed version.

124
00:11:26,350 --> 00:11:30,690
In 1848 a young Louis Pasteur looked at crystals under

125
00:11:30,710 --> 00:11:33,210
a microscope and saw two shapes,

126
00:11:33,390 --> 00:11:34,970
mirror images of each other.

127
00:11:35,430 --> 00:11:38,250
He separated them by hand, with tweezers.

128
00:11:38,550 --> 00:11:41,930
Dissolved in water, one turned polarised light one

129
00:11:41,950 --> 00:11:44,810
way, and the other turned it the opposite way,

130
00:11:44,830 --> 00:11:46,730
by exactly the same amount.

131
00:12:07,792 --> 00:12:11,532
Ordinary chemistry makes both hands in equal amounts.

132
00:12:11,632 --> 00:12:13,052
Life does not.

133
00:12:13,232 --> 00:12:16,972
Almost every amino acid in your proteins is left-handed.

134
00:12:17,152 --> 00:12:19,852
The sugars in your DNA are right-handed,

135
00:12:19,872 --> 00:12:22,492
and the double helix twists to the right.

136
00:12:37,897 --> 00:12:40,597
Life chose one hand and stuck with it.

137
00:12:40,857 --> 00:12:43,477
How that first choice was made is still an open

138
00:12:43,497 --> 00:12:46,677
question. But without it, the parts of a cell could

139
00:12:46,697 --> 00:12:49,557
not fit together, like trying to shake a right hand

140
00:12:49,577 --> 00:12:50,577
with a left.

141
00:13:05,122 --> 00:13:08,542
Break four. You began as a single cell,

142
00:13:08,642 --> 00:13:10,062
roughly a ball.

143
00:13:10,322 --> 00:13:13,102
A ball looks the same from every direction.

144
00:13:13,322 --> 00:13:16,062
So how does it decide where the head goes?

145
00:13:27,704 --> 00:13:31,524
In 1952 Alan Turing, the codebreaker,

146
00:13:31,544 --> 00:13:34,084
wrote a paper about exactly this.

147
00:13:34,344 --> 00:13:37,244
He showed that an even mix of chemicals that react

148
00:13:37,264 --> 00:13:40,724
and spread can become unstable, precisely because

149
00:13:40,744 --> 00:13:44,164
they spread, and settle all by itself into spots and

150
00:13:44,184 --> 00:13:48,244
stripes. Tiny random differences get amplified,

151
00:13:48,264 --> 00:13:50,564
just like the lumps in the early universe.

152
00:14:13,796 --> 00:14:16,976
Processes like that, together with cues already in

153
00:14:16,996 --> 00:14:18,896
the egg, lay out a body:

154
00:14:19,156 --> 00:14:21,616
head and tail, back and belly.

155
00:14:21,956 --> 00:14:25,296
About five hundred and fifty-five million years ago,

156
00:14:25,396 --> 00:14:27,856
some of the first animals built like this,

157
00:14:28,036 --> 00:14:31,136
small worm-like creatures, crawled across the sea

158
00:14:31,156 --> 00:14:32,976
floor and left their tracks.

159
00:14:33,236 --> 00:14:36,336
A front, a back, and one mirror line down the

160
00:14:36,356 --> 00:14:39,856
middle. That body plan leads all the way to you.

161
00:15:08,084 --> 00:15:10,944
But you are not quite mirror symmetric either.

162
00:15:11,124 --> 00:15:12,704
Your heart is on the left,

163
00:15:12,724 --> 00:15:14,384
your liver on the right.

164
00:15:14,404 --> 00:15:15,584
Who decided?

165
00:15:23,769 --> 00:15:26,869
In the early embryo there is a tiny pit called

166
00:15:26,889 --> 00:15:29,829
the node, lined with hair-like cilia.

167
00:15:30,209 --> 00:15:33,029
They all spin clockwise, seen from below,

168
00:15:33,209 --> 00:15:36,069
and because they tilt backwards, they push the fluid

169
00:15:36,089 --> 00:15:37,349
towards the left.

170
00:15:38,009 --> 00:15:41,749
Japanese scientists filmed it happening in 1998.

171
00:15:41,769 --> 00:15:45,429
That gentle current, in a pit smaller than a millimetre,

172
00:15:45,449 --> 00:15:48,629
is the first moment your body knows its left from

173
00:15:48,649 --> 00:15:49,649
its right.

174
00:16:15,765 --> 00:16:18,305
Every so often the flow goes wrong,

175
00:16:18,325 --> 00:16:21,025
and a person is born with their organs mirrored.

176
00:16:21,365 --> 00:16:24,545
It happens to roughly one person in ten thousand,

177
00:16:24,645 --> 00:16:26,465
and many never find out.

178
00:16:39,176 --> 00:16:40,516
So here is the chain.

179
00:16:40,536 --> 00:16:43,316
The laws of physics are symmetric.

180
00:16:43,576 --> 00:16:46,836
Matter beat antimatter by one in a billion.

181
00:16:47,016 --> 00:16:50,356
The smooth universe got lumpy and made stars.

182
00:16:50,536 --> 00:16:52,116
Life picked a hand.

183
00:16:52,216 --> 00:16:54,836
A round cell picked a head and a tail.

184
00:16:54,896 --> 00:16:57,316
And a whirl of cilia put your heart on the

185
00:16:57,336 --> 00:16:58,336
left.

186
00:17:17,588 --> 00:17:20,288
Symmetry gives the world its rules.

187
00:17:20,468 --> 00:17:23,248
Breaking it gives the world its stories.

188
00:17:23,508 --> 00:17:26,608
You are made of five broken symmetries.

189
00:17:37,603 --> 00:17:39,023
Now it is your turn.

190
00:17:39,043 --> 00:17:42,463
Find something near you with perfect symmetry,

191
00:17:42,483 --> 00:17:45,903
and something that is almost symmetric but not quite.

192
00:17:46,163 --> 00:17:48,943
Which one tells you more about how it was made?

193
00:18:01,125 --> 00:18:04,545
You can play with symmetry yourself in Symmetry Engine.

194
00:18:04,725 --> 00:18:08,265
Build a perfect pattern, then break one rule and watch

195
00:18:08,285 --> 00:18:11,865
what grows. The symmetry playlist has the snowflake,

196
00:18:11,885 --> 00:18:13,665
the universe and Escher.

197
00:18:13,925 --> 00:18:16,425
Tell me in the comments which break surprised you

198
00:18:16,445 --> 00:18:18,705
most. I read every comment.
